hume-dialogues-1.md:1: argue/readings: the conclusion is read 2 ways: hume-dialogues-1.logic.r1 has "wide → suspense ∧ triumph"; hume-dialogues-1.logic.r1.2 and hume-dialogues-1.logic.r1.3 have "wide → triumph"; they differ when only "our arguments lose this advantage and run wide of common life" and "scepticism triumphs" are true, which the second allows
hume-dialogues-1.md:1: argue/answered: the conclusion: answered no, which keeps hume-dialogues-1.logic.r1, to: Suppose "our arguments lose this advantage and run wide of common life" and "scepticism triumphs" are true and "the mind remains in suspense between them" is false. Is the conclusion, "The mind must remain in suspense between them; and it is that very suspense or balance, which is the triumph of scepticism.", true then?
